The force between two point charges q and q,, separated by a distance r in free space, is written by using Eq. (10.2),
\begin{split} F = &amp; \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0} \times \frac{q_1q_2}{r^2} = 9 \times 10^9 \times \frac{q_1q_2}{r^2} \\ \varepsilon_0 = &amp; 8.85 \times 10^{-12} \text{ C}^2 \text{ N}^{-1} \text{ m}^{-2} \\ \text{If} \qquad q_1 = &amp; q_2 = &amp; 1\text{C and } r = 1.0 \text{ m} \end{split}
If q_1 = q_2 = 1C and r = 1.0 m
Then F = 9.0 \times 10^{9}
From this, we define, coulomb (C) the unit of charge in SI units.
One coulomb is the amount of charge which, when placed at a distance of one metre from another charge of the same magnitude in vacuum, experiences a force of 9.0 \times 10^9 N. This force is a tremendously large force realisable in practical situations. It is, therefore, necessary to express the charge in smaller units for practical purpose. Subunits of coulomb are used in electrostatics. For example, micro-coulomb (10&lt;sup&gt;-6&lt;/sup&gt; C, C), nanocoulomb (10&lt;sup&gt;-9&lt;/sup&gt; C, nC) or pico-coulomb. (10&lt;sup&gt;-12&lt;/sup&gt; C, pC) are normally used units.

