Example 10.6: Three point charges are placed at the vertices of a right isosceles triangle as shown in the Fig. a. What is the magnitude and direction of the resultant electric field at point P which is the mid point of its hypotenuse?
Solution: Electric field is the force an a unit positive charge, the fields at P due to the charges at A, B and C are shown in the Fig. b. \vec{E}_A is the field at P due to charge at A and \vec{E}_C is the field at P due to charge at C. Since P is the midpoint of AC and the fields at A and C are equal in
magnitudes and are opposite in direction, \vec{E}_A = -\vec{E}_C. \vec{E}_A + \vec{E}_C = 0. Thus, the field at P is only to the charge at B and can be written as
\begin{split} \vec{E}_{p} &amp;= \vec{E}_{B} = \frac{2 \times 10^{-6}}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0} (BP)^{2}} \\ \vec{E}_{p} &amp;= \frac{2 \times 10^{-6} \times 9 \times 10^{9}}{(5 / \sqrt{2})^{2}} \\ &amp;= \frac{2 \times 9 \times 10^{3} \times 2}{25} \\ &amp;= \frac{36}{25} \times 10^{3} \\ &amp;= 1.44 \times 10^{15} \text{ NC}^{-1} \text{ along } \overline{BP} \end{split}
To calculate BP
\left| \overrightarrow{BP} \right| = (BA) \cos (45)^{\circ} = \frac{5}{\sqrt{2}}
Example 10.7: A simplified model of hydrogen atom consists of an electron revolving about a proton at a distance of 5.3 \times 10^{-11}m. The charge on a proton is +1.6 \times 10^{-19} C. Calculate the intensity of the electric field due to proton at this distance.
Solution:
E = \frac{q}{4\pi\varepsilon_o r^2}
q = +1.6 \times 10^{-19} C
r = 5.3 \times 10^{-11} m
\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_o} = 9.0 \times 10^9 Nm^2 C^{-1}
E = \frac{1.6 \times 10^{-19}}{(5.3 \times 10^{-11})^2} \times 9.0 \times 10^9 = 5.1 \times 10^{11} NC^{-1}
The force between electron and proton in hydrogen atom can be calculated by using the electric field. We have, E = \frac{F}{L} : F = qE
F = -1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C} \times 5.1 \times 10^{11} \text{ NC}^{-1}= -8.16 \times 10^{8} \text{ N}.
This force is attractive.
Using the Coulomb's law,
F = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0} \frac{q_1 q_2}{r^2}
= 9.0 \times 10^9 Nm^2 C^{-1} \times \frac{(-1.6 \times 10^{-19} C) \times (+1.6 \times 10^{-19} C)}{(5.3 \times 10^{-11} m)^2}
= -8.6 \times 10^8 N
Knowing electric field at a point is useful to estimate the force experienced by a charge at that point.
